  "slug": "microbe-comprehensive-mapping-583d",
  "summary": "New bacterial studies pair genetic interaction screens, structure- and coevolution-based annotation, and ML-parameterized metabolic models to map function at genome scale.",
  "synthesis": "The items converge on genome-scale mapping of bacterial function, combining experimental and computational layers. A dual-sgRNA CRISPRi platform maps essential-gene genetic interactions in M. smegmatis and M. tuberculosis. Annotation work spans DeepEST, which integrates expression, operon-aware gene location and predicted structures for GO assignment; ContextMirror2.0, which predicts species-specific interactomes by coevolution in E. coli, S. enterica and S. aureus; and a structure/function pipeline annotating the UTI89 proteome that surfaces host-microbe and microbe-phage pathways. On the modeling side, kinGEMs uses deep learning-predicted kcat values with uncertainty-aware tuning to build enzyme-constrained models for 93 organisms, and GEM-CM couples random-forest surrogates of genome-scale models to reaction-diffusion simulation of P. aeruginosa PA14 in gels. The common novelty is learned surrogates and predictors scaling mechanistic or annotation layers.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-08"
